\section{IMPLEMENTATION IN PRACTICE}

\subsection{Pedagogical strategies}
Even though the framework, as mentioned before, should only give an open reciepe for the teacher in order to build the class contents, there are several pedagogical strategies that we have identified through which the framework might take shape in the classroom:

- Opening black boxes: A oundational practice that consists in bringing students into contact with the interior of the tools they use, understanding their aplications and implications. This method, tnough, does not intend the students to become experts,; instead, it means offering enough conceptual transparency for the learner to understand the tools and how to use them under theier non-frictional interfacde.

- Documenting thinking processes: In orther to mitigate the externalization of the student's own reasoning, making it visible becomes imperative. In order to achieve this, documentation presents a viable way of doing so. Documentation in this context does imply not only the outputs arrived to but the path taken to get there, questions asked along it and the frictions faced as well as the responses done to them.

- Structured transparency in AI use: Alongside the documentation of thinking processes, transparency protocols that reveal the use of LLMs in the different tasks could be of great utility, not only as a way of being "transparent" with the teacher but also as a way of selfreflecting on the usage done, promoting in this case a "honest reflection, responsible use of AI, and clarity in collaborative, tech-assisted creation" \cite{fuentemilla2024ccl}

- assumption mapping [super important, supersuper important anda good way of establishing a first step for metacognitive thinking]

- system analysis tasks [this could be a positive approach in order to enable and continue the metacognitive as an established map of interconnected aspects which play into the task at handd]

It's worth noting that these exposed proposals are just that, proposals, in order to appropiately put them into practice or even discard them in favour of others, they should be evaluated and tested in their respective educational context.

\subsection{Learning environments}

Regardless of the strategies adopted, it is of great importance to emphasize that guaranteeing a correct and responsible integration of LLMs in the classroom implies the participation of all teachers. Just as digital literacy was never solely the responsibility of the informatics teacher, nor was writing the exclusive domain of the language teacher, critical engagement with LLMs belongs across the curriculum. 
A math teacher discussing the reliability of AI generated responses to a math problem, a history teacher questioning the biases in how an LLM frames a historical event, or a language teacher analyzing why chatbots do not construct sentences correctly in catalan language are situations that through transversal implementation trigger critical inquiry.

This distributed model of engagement can be facilitated via the implementation of project-based and service-based learning environments, where boundaries between disciplines get dissolved around a shared problem or goal. When students work towards a real output, whether a designed object or a community intervention, the question of which tools to use, how to use them, and what their limitations arise organically. Academany programs, based on making, documenting, and iterating display this integration of disciplines by requiring students to be able to formulate questions, confront uncertainty, understand tools and reflect on one's own process. In these environments, no single instructor holds authority over the full range of knowledge required to develop one's own final project, the student must navigate disciplines, ask peers, and test tools in order to get to their goal.

\subsection{Educating educators}
Under this framework, it becomes central for teachers to adopt an epistemic rather than instrumental understanding of AI. Yet existing guidance documents usually fall short in this aspect. As an example, the Catalan Department of Education's guidance on AI in schools \cite{gencat2024ia}, offers a useful starting point, acknowledging that teacher training is an essential element, and that its goal should be to allow educators to make effective use of AI both in their professional tasks and in the classroom. Even though it also explains in general lines the risks of the technology such as bias, excessive trust, and loss of autonomy, its approach remains mainly instrumental, focused on preparing teachers to use and manage AI applications, rather than to properly understand and question them. In order to go further this research wonders how can educators empower critical thinking about LLMs if they themselves take a "black box" approach to the technology?

On this aspect, this research finds technical mentality \cite{benabdallah2024technicalmentality}, extremely useful to ideate what a deeper, non-instrumental understanding of AI might look like in education. Technical mentality proposes that engaging with the inner workings of technical systems is the main condition to develop more critical relationships with technology. It is important to highlight though that this does not mean educators must become experts in the architecture of LLMs, it does mean that teachers should cultivate in their practice a disposition of inquiry towards the technology, critically questioning within their own disciplinarity.


